      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Quayholt Insurance, a fictional company. A colleague, Ayaan, clicked a convincing email and signed in, and now his account is being used. You're covering his inbox at the kiosk. Some of what turns up is a real sign that something is wrong, and some is ordinary. Sort it, and report or check anything that doesn't sit right. Reporting when you're unsure is always fine and never marked down.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "Nothing to replay this time",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "It landed clean, with nothing in it to catch this time. Next, you're the one whose account is being used, sorting the signs that follow.",
        "noBlameLine": "Everyone clicks something eventually, and a colleague who reports their own slip is doing exactly the right thing. A miss here just shows where to look next time; nobody is in trouble for reporting.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: if you think you have clicked, signed in or spotted something odd on your account or a colleague's, report it straight away, change your password on the real site, and leave the device as it is. Reporting fast, even your own slip, is what limits the damage, and report it using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "These signs were set off by an email built to look right, and anyone can be caught by one. Anything worth another look is shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "conceptPanel": [
          {
            "heading": "The five questions a report answers",
            "text": "A useful report answers five questions, as far as you know: what you noticed, when, where (which account, device or system), who is affected, and what you have done so far. Be honest about the last one: 'I clicked the link', 'I signed in', 'I restarted it'. A rough report now beats a perfect one later; the team will ask for the rest."
          },
          {
            "heading": "First steps and recovery",
            "text": "If you clicked or signed in: report it, change the password on the real site (not through any link), then change it anywhere you used the same one, and follow the service desk. For anything on a device: leave it as it is. Do not switch it off unless you are told to, do not delete the evidence, and do not try to fix it. If the service desk asks, unplug the network cable or turn off Wi-Fi and leave it on."
          }
        ],
        "defendIntro": "Go through the signs in the colleague's inbox and decide what to do with each one. Some are a real sign that someone else is in the account, and some are ordinary; the one move that works for every real sign is to report it and let the team act. Take them at your own pace."
      },
      "debrief": {
        "mondayCard": "slot:customer_report_route",
        "noBlameLine": true
      }
    },
    "awareness": {
      "ruleOfThumb": {
        "heading": "The habit to keep",
        "text": "When a message pressures you to move money, grant access, sign in or read back a code, stop and check it yourself through a route you already trust, not the link, number or page the message gives you. Taking a minute to confirm is always a fair thing to do."
      }
